<b><font size=4>PLANTING IN THE RAIN</font></b><br>
Challenges of a Wet Meadow in Early Summer
<p>
<b>Denny Dawes</b><br>
<i>Wildlife Habitat Institute (WHI)</i>
<p>
The planting season began on June 11th with Kurt Dostal, Restoration Manager at WHI, hauling two pickup and trailer loads of willow cuttings to Red River.  The willows were placed in the cooler at the USFS Red River Ranger Station and stored until ready for planting the following week. On Monday, June 15th, Denny and Kurt moved the 6-wheeler (ATV), track excavator, and other planting equipment to Red River.  Willows were transported from the USFS cooler to the on-site wetland for soaking.  The mini-excavator was used to auger holes for the willow poles.  The Americorps volunteer crew of nine assisted with planting willows all day June 17th and one half-day on June 18th.  WHI's new 6-wheeler performed beautifully.  It thrived in the wet conditions and became indispensable not only as an equipment/willow transport vehicle but also as an Americorps volunteer taxi service.  In fact, one of the volunteers asked, "Is there anything this 6-wheeler won't do? . . . I suppose the airplane adapter kit is sold separately?"  Despite the amazing 6-wheeler and the efficiency of the track excavator, Mother Nature would not be reckoned with - continual rains slowed progress and spirits, torrential rain and 36<sup>o</sup>F ended work at noon on Friday, June 19th.
<p>
<center>
<font size=2>Kurt Drives the infamous 6-wheeler (without the airplane adapter kit)</font>
<img src="six-wheeler.jpg" width=400 height=190 alt="" border="1">
</center>
<p>
Approximately 2,100 willow cuttings were planted the first week.
<p>
During the week of June 22nd, Kurt continued to auger willow holes with the track excavator.  A total of approximately 3,000 willows were placed by June 30th.
<p>
The track excavator with auger attachment turned out to be a real time-saver, as anticipated.  Denny estimated that planting took one-half the time compared to last year. There was an intermittent problem with the auger bits breaking (3 separate times) but, with a spare auger and the help of an efficient welder at Betty's in Elk City, activities proceeded with minimal delays.
<p>
Rains continued throughout the second week of activities as well - 3.5 inches fell from June 15th - 24th  (Elk City Ranger Station); 5 inches fell from June 1st - 15th; rained hard and long June 25th - 26th.
<p>
Special thanks to the Johnsons, our downstream neighbors, for allowing 
us access to the west side of the meadow via their road. 

